\alias{LearnerRegrFuser}
\title{Fused Lasso Regression Learner}
\description{
Fused lasso regression over subsets of the data.
One linear model is fitted per subset, and an L2 fusion penalty shares information between the coefficients
of the subsets.
Calls \code{fuser::generateBlockDiagonalMatrices()} and \code{fuser::fusedL2DescentGLMNet()} from \CRANpkg{fuser}.
}
\section{Subsets}{

The learner requires a task with exactly one column with the column role \code{"subset"}, which assigns each row to
a subset.
The \code{"subset"} role is registered by \CRANpkg{mlr3resampling}, so that package has to be loaded before the role
can be set, e.g. with \code{task$set_col_roles("column", roles = "subset")}.
There must be at least two subsets in the training data, and predictions are only possible for subsets that
were present during training.
The \code{"group"} role is not used because mlr3 keeps the rows of a group together when resampling,
so that the test set would only contain subsets that the model has not seen.
}

\section{Initial parameter values}{

\itemize{
\item \code{lambda} is initialized to 0.01, because the upstream function has no default for it.
The value should be tuned.
\item \code{scaling} is initialized to \code{FALSE}, the upstream default is \code{TRUE}.
With scaling, the features and the target of each subset are divided by the number of rows of the subset,
so that a suitable \code{lambda} depends strongly on the number of rows and the initial value would shrink
all coefficients to zero on all but very small data sets.
}
}

\section{Custom mlr3 parameters}{

\itemize{
\item \code{G} is the matrix of pairwise fusion strengths between the subsets.
Rows and columns are aligned with the subset names, which are converted to character and sorted.
If \code{G} is not set, all pairs of subsets are fused with strength 1.
\item \code{intercept} fits one unpenalized intercept per subset if \code{TRUE}.
The intercept that \code{glmnet::glmnet()} would fit across all subsets is always disabled,
because the upstream function discards it.
\item \code{lambda} must be a single value, the upstream function accepts a vector.
}
}

